Alright, let’s optimize prompt crafting so your CPU-only generations look rich and detailed even with only ~20 steps. The key is precision and layering, not length. Here are some techniques:

	1. Layer your prompt: Think in terms of Subject → Environment → Style → Mood/Lighting → Detail. Example:

		Subject: “a medieval wizard”
		Environment: “in a stone tower, surrounded by books and glowing runes”
		Style: “digital painting, concept art”
		Mood/Lighting: “cinematic lighting, misty atmosphere”
		Detail: “ultra-detailed, sharp focus, intricate textures”



		Full prompt:

		a medieval wizard in a stone tower surrounded by books and glowing runes, digital painting, cinematic lighting, misty atmosphere, ultra-detailed, intricate textures, sharp focus

		The order matters subtly. SD tends to emphasize first tokens more, so put the core subject first, modifiers after.



	2. Use negative prompts:

	Prevent common CPU annoyances like low-res artifacts. Examples:

		blurry, low quality, watermark, text, deformed anatomy

	Always include this — it’s like a filter for bad iterations. On CPU, this is your shortcut to fewer wasted images.



	3. Weight your keywords:

	Parentheses and colons can amplify or reduce emphasis:

		(glowing runes:1.5) → more prominent
		(wizard:0.8) → slightly de-emphasized


	Example:

		(a medieval wizard:1.2) in a stone tower, (glowing runes:1.5), digital painting, cinematic lighting

	This lets you guide the model precisely without changing the whole prompt.


	4. Style references:

	Instead of vague adjectives, reference styles or artists. SD knows these better:

		“by Greg Rutkowski” → often increases fantasy illustration detail
		“studio Ghibli style” → for soft, whimsical textures
		“cinematic lighting” → sharp highlights and shadows
		“photorealistic, 8k” → increases micro-detail


	5. Experiment with framing and composition:

		“Close-up portrait of…”
		“Wide-angle view of…”
		“From above / bird’s-eye view”
		“Foreground focus with blurred background”


	Composition words dramatically affect perceived detail and clarity, especially on limited steps.


	6. Keep it concise but layered:

	CPU steps are limited; overloading the prompt can confuse the model. Aim for 5–10 strong modifiers. Fewer, high-impact 
	words often outperform long rambling prompts.
